  "prompt_text": "A scientific review-style illustration about mitochondrial rejuvenation and aging. At the top, show an old mitochondrion in pale gray gradually shifting into a younger and healthier mitochondrion in warmer colors, with an arrow labeled rejuvenation. Add a small callout nearby listing possible ways to promote this transition, including caloric restriction, partial reprogramming, heterochronic parabiosis, and pharmaceutical treatment.Below this, create the main schematic showing communication between the mitochondrion and the nucleus. The mitochondrion should appear as a source of important metabolites and signaling molecules, such as NAD+, α-KG and other TCA intermediates, SAM, acetyl-CoA, and possibly ROS. Use dashed lines or subtle arrows to connect these molecules to the nucleus, suggesting that mitochondrial metabolism can influence nuclear regulation.Inside or around the nucleus, include several classes of gene-regulatory enzymes and chromatin factors, including sirtuins, histone demethylases and TET enzymes, DNA methyltransferases, histone acetyltransferases, and Rph1p. The figure should make it clear that these mitochondrial metabolites affect the activity of nuclear epigenetic and transcriptional regulators.Also show a feedback connection from the nucleus back to the mitochondrion, indicating that changes in nuclear regulation can alter the expression of mitochondrial genes and reshape mitochondrial function. Overall, the image should convey a clear feedback loop between mitochondrial metabolism and nuclear control, highlighting this reciprocal relationship as a key idea in mitochondrial rejuvenation and aging..",
